window.docContents[175].resolve({"/Maps/#section-embeddings-of-hypercubes":{"contents":"There are two natural embeddings of Q[n] into Q[n + 1], given by sending a vertex\nv to (v, false) or (v, true). These will be very useful when we start looking\nat walks in the hypercube. We can define these in terms of the boxProdLeft graph\nembedding.\n\n#check boxProdLeft --  G ↪g G □ H\n\n\nThe embedding Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1], with v ↦ (v, b) (where b : Bool)./-- The embedding Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1], with v ↦ (v, b) (where b : Bool). -/\n@[simps!]\ndef hypercube.embedBool (n : ℕ) (b : Bool) : Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1] :=\n  Q[n].boxProdLeft _ b\n\n\nThe embedding Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1], with v ↦ (v, false)./-- The embedding Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1], with v ↦ (v, false). -/\nabbrev hypercube.embedFalse (n : ℕ) : Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1] :=\n  hypercube.embedBool n false\n\n\nThe embedding Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1], with v ↦ (v, true)./-- The embedding Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1], with v ↦ (v, true). -/\nabbrev hypercube.embedTrue (n : ℕ) : Q[n] ↪g Q[n + 1] :=\n  hypercube.embedBool n true\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Hypercubes and Mycielskians","header":"5.5.1. Embeddings of Hypercubes","id":"/Maps/#section-embeddings-of-hypercubes"}});